Project context for this specification — mock3:

- The only codeline in scope is `mocka`, checked out at `/home/bradleyjerome/projects/mock3/mock-a`. Its declared dependencies are `vitest` and `typescript` and nothing else. Any acceptance criterion that would require a runtime, framework, database, HTTP layer or build tool beyond those two is out of scope for this project — write the criterion against plain TypeScript modules and `vitest` tests instead.
- The repository is very small (a handful of files). Prefer acceptance criteria that name the exact module and exported function under test over criteria phrased at the level of "the system" or "the service". In a repo this size a specification that stays abstract cannot be verified.
- Two tickets are in scope and they are not the same shape:
  - MOCK3-1 (riders aged exactly 65 charged the adult fare) is a confirmed boundary-condition defect in the age comparison inside `src/fares.ts`, reachable through `fareFor()`. It is a source edit. The specification should pin the boundary explicitly — the age value at the boundary, the fare category expected at that value, and the categories expected immediately either side of it — so that an off-by-one in the other direction cannot pass.
  - MOCK3-2 (the departure board omits the final stop) has no confirmed surface in this repository. If the evidence blocks above do not locate a departure-board module, function or test in `mocka`, do not invent one. Say what is missing in `notes`, and write acceptance criteria that can only be satisfied once the surface is identified, rather than criteria that assume a file name.
- Two roles work this project: `fare-rules-engineer` (implementer — owns source edits to the fare logic) and `mocka-detective` (investigator — read-only confirmation of the boundary condition and of whether any MOCK3-2 surface exists). Write acceptance criteria an implementer can satisfy with a source change plus a `vitest` test, and keep investigation findings in `notes` rather than smuggling them into criteria as if they were requirements.
- TypeScript traps worth pinning in criteria for this stack: boundary comparisons (`<` vs `<=`, `>` vs `>=`) are exactly the bug class MOCK3-1 belongs to, so state the inclusive/exclusive intent in words, not just by example; and `vitest` assertions on numeric fares must state the expected value, not merely that a value was returned.
- Regression coverage belongs in the specification: a fix to the age comparison must come with a `vitest` case at the boundary value itself, because a fix verified only at values away from the boundary is indistinguishable from no fix.

